# PHP Code Standards

## PSR-12 Coding Standards

Follow PSR-12 coding standards strictly for all PHP code.

## Strict Types

- Always use strict types at the top of PHP files:
```php
<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

namespace App\Http\Controllers;
```

## Type Hints

- Use type hints for all parameters
- Use return type declarations for all methods
- Use nullable types when appropriate (`?string`, `?int`)
- Use union types when needed (PHP 8.0+)

**Example:**
```php
public function createUser(string $name, ?string $email = null): User
{
    // ...
}
```

## PHP 8.x Features

### Attributes (PHP 8.0+)
Use attributes instead of PHPDoc annotations where applicable:

```php
#[Route('/users', methods: ['GET'])]
public function index(): JsonResponse
{
    // ...
}

#[OA\Get(operationId: 'getUsers')]
#[OA\Response(response: 200, description: 'List of users')]
public function index(): JsonResponse
{
    // ...
}
```

### Constructor Property Promotion (PHP 8.0+)
```php
// Instead of this:
class UserService
{
    private UserRepository $userRepository;
    private Mailer $mailer;

    public function __construct(UserRepository $userRepository, Mailer $mailer)
    {
        $this->userRepository = $userRepository;
        $this->mailer = $mailer;
    }
}

// Do this:
class UserService
{
    public function __construct(
        private readonly UserRepository $userRepository,
        private readonly Mailer $mailer,
    ) {}
}
```

### Match Expression (PHP 8.0+)
```php
// Instead of switch:
switch ($status) {
    case 'active':
        $label = 'Active';
        break;
    case 'inactive':
        $label = 'Inactive';
        break;
}

// Use match:
$label = match ($status) {
    'active' => 'Active',
    'inactive' => 'Inactive',
    default => 'Unknown',
};
```

### Enums (PHP 8.1+)
```php
enum OrderStatus: string
{
    case PENDING = 'pending';
    case PROCESSING = 'processing';
    case COMPLETED = 'completed';
    case CANCELLED = 'cancelled';

    public function label(): string
    {
        return match ($this) {
            self::PENDING => 'Chờ xử lý',
            self::PROCESSING => 'Đang xử lý',
            self::COMPLETED => 'Hoàn thành',
            self::CANCELLED => 'Đã hủy',
        };
    }
}

// Usage
$status = OrderStatus::PENDING;
echo $status->value; // 'pending'
echo $status->label(); // 'Chờ xử lý'
```

### Fibers (PHP 8.1+)
Not commonly used but good to know for async operations.

### Readonly Properties (PHP 8.1+)
```php
class UserDTO
{
    public function __construct(
        public readonly string $name,
        public readonly string $email,
        public readonly ?string $phone = null,
    ) {}
}
```

### First-Class Callable Syntax (PHP 8.1+)
```php
// Instead of this:
$func = Closure::fromCallable([$controller, 'method']);
$func = [$controller, 'method'](...);

// Use this:
$func = $controller->method(...);
```

### Disjunctive Normal Form Type Normalization (PHP 8.2+)
```php
// Complex union types should be organized for readability
function foo((A&B)|C $arg): (D&F)|(E&F) {}
```

### Constants in Traits (PHP 8.2+)
```php
trait DatabaseConnection
{
    public const DEFAULT_TIMEOUT = 30;
    public const DEFAULT_RETRY_COUNT = 3;
}
```

### Typed Class Constants (PHP 8.3+)
```php
enum Status: string
{
    case DRAFT = 'draft';
    case PUBLISHED = 'published';

    public const DEFAULT = self::DRAFT;
}
```

## Naming Conventions

- Use meaningful variable names
- Use descriptive method names
- Write self-documenting code
- Avoid abbreviations unless widely known
- Use PascalCase for class names
- Use camelCase for variables, methods, and function names
- Use SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE for constants

**Good:**
```php
$userRepository = new UserRepository();
$activeUsers = $userRepository->findActiveUsers();
const MAX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS = 3;
```

**Bad:**
```php
$ur = new UserRepository();
$au = $ur->findAU();
const MAX = 3;
```

**Naming Conventions Table:**
| Type | Convention | Example |
|------|------------|---------|
| Class | PascalCase | `UserController` |
| Method | camelCase | `getUserById()` |
| Variable | camelCase | `$userName` |
| Constant | SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE | `MAX_RETRY` |
| Trait | PascalCase | `HandlesAuthorization` |
| Interface | PascalCase (with `Interface` suffix optional) | `UserRepositoryInterface` |
| Enum | PascalCase | `OrderStatus` |
| Enum Case | PascalCase | `OrderStatus::PENDING` |

## Comments & Documentation

- Write clear, concise comments for complex logic only
- Use PHPDoc blocks for all classes and public methods
- Document parameters, return types, and exceptions
- Keep comments up to date with code changes
- Prefer self-documenting code over comments

**Example:**
```php
/**
 * Create a new user in the system.
 *
 * @param  string  $name  The user's full name
 * @param  string  $email  The user's email address
 * @return User  The created user instance
 * @throws \InvalidArgumentException  If email is invalid
 */
public function createUser(string $name, string $email): User
{
    // Implementation
}
```

**Better - Self-documenting code:**
```php
public function createUser(string $name, string $email): User
{
    $this->validateEmail($email);

    return User::create([
        'name' => $name,
        'email' => $email,
    ]);
}
```

## Error Handling

### Use Exceptions Properly
```php
// Custom Exception
class UserNotFoundException extends Exception
{
    protected $message = 'User not found';
    protected $code = 404;
}

// Throw with context
throw new UserNotFoundException("User with ID {$id} does not exist");
```

### Laravel Exception Handling
```php
// app/Exceptions/Handler.php
public function register(): void
{
    $this->reportable(function (Throwable $e) {
        // Log to external service
    });

    $this->renderable(function (UserNotFoundException $e) {
        return response()->json([
            'success' => false,
            'message' => $e->getMessage(),
        ], 404);
    });
}
```

### Try-Catch Best Practices
```php
// Do catch specific exceptions
try {
    $user = $this->userRepository->findByEmail($email);
} catch (UserNotFoundException $e) {
    Log::warning('Login attempt for non-existent user', ['email' => $email]);
    throw $e;
}

// Don't catch generic Exception
try {
    // code
} catch (Exception $e) { // Never do this!
    // Too broad, masks real issues
}
```

### Result Pattern (Optional but Recommended)
```php
// For operations that can fail
readonly class Result
{
    public function __construct(
        public bool $success,
        public mixed $data = null,
        public ?string $error = null,
    ) {}

    public static function ok(mixed $data): self
    {
        return new self(true, $data);
    }

    public static function fail(string $error): self
    {
        return new self(false, null, $error);
    }
}

// Usage
$result = $this->userService->createUser($data);
if (!$result->success) {
    return response()->json(['error' => $result->error], 400);
}
return $result->data;
```

## Code Quality Principles

- Follow SOLID principles (Single Responsibility, Open/Closed, Liskov Substitution, Interface Segregation, Dependency Inversion)
- Keep methods small and focused (Single Responsibility)
- Write DRY code (Don't Repeat Yourself)
- Prefer composition over inheritance
- Use meaningful abstractions
- Keep dependencies minimal

### SOLID Principles Quick Reference

**Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)**
```php
// Bad - UserService does too much
class UserService
{
    public function createUser($data) { /* create */ }
    public function sendWelcomeEmail($user) { /* send */ }
    public function logUserCreation($user) { /* log */ }
}

// Good - Separate concerns
class UserService { public function createUser($data) { /* create */ } }
class WelcomeEmailService { public function send($user) { /* send */ } }
class AuditLogService { public function log($action, $data) { /* log */ } }
```

**Dependency Injection**
```php
// Bad - Hard dependency
class UserService
{
    private UserRepository $repository;

    public function __construct()
    {
        $this->repository = new UserRepository(); // Don't do this!
    }
}

// Good - Inject dependencies
class UserService
{
    public function __construct(
        private UserRepositoryInterface $repository,
    ) {}
}
```

## Class Structure Order

Organize class members in this order:
1. Traits
2. Constants
3. Properties (public, then protected, then private)
4. Constructor
5. Public methods
6. Protected methods
7. Private methods

```php
class UserController
{
    use AuthorizesRequests, ValidatesRequests;

    private const MAX_ITEMS_PER_PAGE = 20;

    public function __construct(
        private readonly UserService $userService,
    ) {}

    public function index(Request $request): JsonResponse { /* ... */ }
    public function show(int $id): JsonResponse { /* ... */ }

    private function validatePagination(Request $request): array { /* ... */ }
}
```

## Control Structures

### Prefer early returns (guard clauses)
```php
// Bad
public function update(Request $request, User $user): JsonResponse
{
    if ($request->has('name')) {
        if (strlen($request->name) > 0) {
            $user->name = $request->name;
            if ($user->save()) {
                return response()->json(['success' => true]);
            }
        }
    }
    return response()->json(['success' => false], 400);
}

// Good - Early returns
public function update(Request $request, User $user): JsonResponse
{
    if (!$request->has('name') || strlen($request->name) === 0) {
        return response()->json(['success' => false], 400);
    }

    $user->name = $request->name;
    $user->save();

    return response()->json(['success' => true]);
}
```

### Use nullsafe operator (PHP 8.0+)
```php
// Instead of
$length = $user !== null && $user->profile !== null
    ? $user->profile->getNameLength()
    : null;

// Use
$length = $user?->profile?->getNameLength();
```

### Use match expression (PHP 8.0+)
See PHP 8.x Features section.

## Boolean Evaluation

### Explicit comparisons
```php
// Bad
if ($isActive) { /* ... */ }
if (!$user) { /* ... */ }

// Good
if ($isActive === true) { /* ... */ }
if ($isActive === false) { /* ... */ }
if ($user === null) { /* ... */ }
if ($user !== null) { /* ... */ }
```

### Check array key existence
```php
// Instead of
if ($data['key']) { /* may fail if value is falsy */ }

// Use
if (isset($data['key'])) { /* ... */ }
if (array_key_exists('key', $data)) { /* ... */ }
```

